<div class="col hero">
  <h1>Toolbelt &amp; Trowel</h1>
  <p class="hero__lead">A plain-spoken workshop journal for keeping a home and yard in good order. Clear, safe, step-by-step guides to the repairs worth doing yourself, the simple projects worth building, and the easy pleasure of growing in containers — always with honest, plain-spoken advice on when to roll up your sleeves and when to call a professional instead.</p>
</div>
<figure class="figure figure--hero col">
  <img src="assets/img/home-hero.webp" alt="A tidy workbench with hand tools arranged on it." width="1024" height="683">
  <figcaption class="figure__cap"> <span class="figure__credit">Photo: danielmee33 / flickr (<a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/8432632@N04/28260557796" rel="noopener nofollow">CC BY 2.0</a>)</span></figcaption>
</figure>

<div class="wrap">
  <h2 id="start">Where would you like to begin today?</h2>
  <p>Every guide is written in the same plain, patient way: what you will need, what to do, the safety steps that matter, and — on every repair — our honest take on whether it is a job for you or for a professional. Nothing here assumes you are an expert, and nothing pushes you past what is safe.</p>
  <ul class="cardgrid"><li class="card"><div class="badge"><span>Easy</span></div><h3><a href="home-repair-guides.html">Home Repairs</a></h3><p>Patch drywall, clear a drain, replace a toilet flapper, and weatherstrip a door — the fixes that save the most on callouts.</p></li><li class="card"><div class="badge"><span>Easy–Medium</span></div><h3><a href="diy-projects.html">DIY Projects</a></h3><p>Build a simple shelf, plan safe grab bars, and freshen a tired porch, one satisfying step at a time.</p></li><li class="card"><div class="badge"><span>Start here</span></div><h3><a href="tool-basics-and-safety.html">Tools &amp; Safety</a></h3><p>The handful of tools worth owning, how to use them safely, and the gear that keeps your hands and eyes protected.</p></li><li class="card"><div class="badge"><span>Easy</span></div><h3><a href="lawn-and-yard.html">Lawn &amp; Yard</a></h3><p>Mowing, trimming, leaf cleanup, and the seasonal yard jobs that keep the outside of a home tidy.</p></li><li class="card"><div class="badge"><span>Easy</span></div><h3><a href="container-gardening.html">Container Gardening</a></h3><p>Grow herbs, tomatoes, and flowers in pots on a patio or step — no digging, no bending to the ground.</p></li></ul>

  <hr class="gridrule" aria-hidden="true">

  <div class="col">
    <h2 id="why-diy">The quiet rewards of doing it yourself</h2>
    <p>There is a particular pride in a job done with your own hands. A shelf you built holds your books a little more sweetly for being yours; a drip you silenced stops nagging at you every time you pass the sink. Beyond the satisfaction, there is real money saved — many of the jobs a handyman charges a good sum for are genuinely simple once someone shows you how, and the few dollars of materials are a fraction of a callout fee.</p>
    <p>Doing small jobs yourself also keeps you independent and keeps your home promptly cared for, rather than waiting weeks for someone to come and fix a five-minute problem. And the skills build on one another: patch one wall and the next feels easy; silence one hinge and you will never dread the sound again. Confidence, like a good tool kit, is assembled one piece at a time, and this workshop is here to help you gather both.</p>
    <p>None of which means doing everything yourself. The wisest homeowners are not the ones who tackle every job, but the ones who know exactly which jobs to tackle and which to hand to a professional — and who feel equally good about both choices.</p>

    <h2 id="philosophy">A few honest words up front</h2>
    <p>There is a deep satisfaction in fixing a thing yourself. A silenced drip, a patched wall, a shelf that holds steady — these small victories make a home feel like yours, and they save real money on callouts for jobs that are genuinely simple. We are here to help you win those small victories with confidence.</p>
    <p>But we are just as committed to the other half of the truth: knowing when <em>not</em> to do it yourself. Some jobs carry real danger or real cost if they go wrong, and no amount of thrift is worth a shock, a gas leak, a flood behind a wall, or a fall from a roof. So on every repair guide you will find a clear table — our honest take on whether to pick up a tool or pick up the phone. We would far rather you called a professional for the right job than attempted something that could harm you or your home.</p>
    <p>That balance — can-do confidence paired with plain good sense about limits — is the whole spirit of this workshop. A good tradesperson knows exactly what they can and cannot do, and that is the mindset we hope to pass along.</p>

    <h2 id="for-beginners">Never done this sort of thing before?</h2>
    <p>Then you are exactly who we wrote this for. Everybody who is handy today was once a beginner holding a screwdriver the wrong way round, and the plain truth is that most household jobs are far simpler than they appear from the outside. The trick is to start with the easy wins — a squeaky hinge, a drafty door, a shelf — and let each small success build your confidence for the next. You do not need talent, only patience and a willingness to read the steps and go slowly.</p>
    <p>Every guide here assumes no prior knowledge. We explain each tool the first time it appears, we break each job into clear steps, and we tell you plainly what to do if something looks different from what we describe. And we never, ever push you toward a job that is unsafe for a beginner — that is what the "call a pro" guidance is for. Start small, take your time, and you will surprise yourself with how much you can do.</p>

    <h2 id="comfort">Built for comfortable working</h2>
    <p>We write with every reader in mind, and with older hands especially. We favor the methods that are easier on the back, the knees, and the grip — the job done sitting down where it can be, the lighter tool, the approach that avoids the ladder. Where a task really needs strength, reach, or balance that has become difficult, we say so plainly and suggest a helping hand, because there is wisdom, not weakness, in knowing your limits.</p>
    <p>Toolbelt &amp; Trowel is an independent home-and-yard workshop journal. Everything here is free to read, we sell nothing, and we recommend no brands — when we name a product type, it is only to teach. You can enlarge the type with a tap, every photograph is a real one, and the guidance is kept plain and practical. We have no shop, no affiliate links, and no stake in anything but seeing your home and yard well kept. Pull on your work gloves and begin wherever you like; <a href="tool-basics-and-safety.html">Tools &amp; Safety</a> is a sound place to start.</p>
